# The fields of REData, the open data of Red Eléctrica, the operator of the Spanish electricity grid, at
# apidatos.ree.es, in English. Every indicator is one path, /{category}/{widget}, and answers in the same JSON:API
# shape, so a generator wrote one field for each of the 68 widgets that answered on 23 October 2026, from the list on
# the documentation page of the API, and one set of types from the recorded answers. The descriptions are written by
# hand from the series each answer holds. The figures are for the whole of Spain or the peninsula. Red Eléctrica blocks
# an address that sends many requests in a row, so a field waits for its answer before the next one is sent. Each field
# is fetched by a RestOperationFetcher, see ReeConfiguration.

extend type Query {
    # An indicator answers a list of series, such as the generation of each technology, and each series holds its values
    # over the period asked for, one for each hour, day, month or year. The values of energy are in MWh, of power in MW,
    # and of prices in euros per MWh.

    """
    The balance of electricity in Spain: the generation of each renewable and non-renewable technology, the storage in
    pumped hydro and batteries, the cross-border exchange balance and the demand at busbars, for each day or month
    """
    reeElectricityBalance(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. DAY, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = DAY
    ): ReeIndicator

    "The demand for electricity in Spain over time, in MWh"
    reeDemand(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. DAY, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = DAY
    ): ReeIndicator

    """
    How the demand for electricity changed against the same period of the year before, and its components: the effect of
    working days, of temperature, and the adjusted demand. The components add up to the variation.
    """
    reeDemandVariation(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. MONTH, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = MONTH
    ): ReeIndicator

    """
    How the demand for electricity of the last twelve months changed against the twelve months before, with its
    components: working days, temperature and adjusted demand
    """
    reeDemandVariationMovingYear(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. MONTH, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = MONTH
    ): ReeIndicator

    """
    The Red Eléctrica index (IRE) tells early how the electricity consumption of the companies with more than 450 kW
    contracted evolves, as an index with 2021 = 100 and its growth rate, raw and corrected for working days and
    temperature. This field gives the general index and its monthly variation.
    """
    reeConsumptionIndex(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. MONTH, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = MONTH
    ): ReeIndicator

    """
    The Red Eléctrica index (IRE) tells early how the electricity consumption of the companies with more than 450 kW
    contracted evolves, as an index with 2021 = 100 and its growth rate, raw and corrected for working days and
    temperature. This field gives the general index and its variation against a year before.
    """
    reeConsumptionIndexYearly(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. MONTH, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = MONTH
    ): ReeIndicator

    """
    The Red Eléctrica index (IRE) tells early how the electricity consumption of the companies with more than 450 kW
    contracted evolves, as an index with 2021 = 100 and its growth rate, raw and corrected for working days and
    temperature. This field gives the general index of the last twelve months.
    """
    reeConsumptionIndexMovingYear(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. MONTH, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = MONTH
    ): ReeIndicator

    """
    The Red Eléctrica index (IRE) tells early how the electricity consumption of the companies with more than 450 kW
    contracted evolves, as an index with 2021 = 100 and its growth rate, raw and corrected for working days and
    temperature. This field gives the index of industry and its monthly variation.
    """
    reeIndustryConsumptionIndex(
        "The first day of the period, such as 2026-10-01, in the time of Madrid"
        startDate: Date!
        "The last day of the period, such as 2026-10-20. It can be the same day as startDate."
        endDate: Date!
        "How the values are grouped. MONTH, the default, is one this indicator accepts."
        timeTrunc: ReeTimeTrunc! = MONTH
